# Firstmate's own critical surfaces, for bin/fm-lane-lib.sh.
#
# Format: "<glob> <surface-name>", one per line. Globs are matched
# case-insensitively against the repo-relative path of every changed file.
# This file only ADDS to the built-in catalog in bin/fm-lane-lib.sh; it can
# never remove a built-in surface, so nothing here can lower rigor.
#
# What belongs here: the paths whose names the portable built-in patterns do not
# already catch, but whose failure mode is a safety boundary rather than a bug -
# work discarded, a branch landed that should not have, a guard stepped around,
# a lock or lease that stops protecting what it names. Add a path here when it
# joins that set; never remove one to make a delivery cheaper.
#
# It also carries the two boundaries this house raises for itself rather than
# imposing on every project through the portable catalog: this repo's CI
# configuration, which is what the cheap lane leans on, and the shared tracked
# material AGENTS.md section 1 already requires ship through the no-mistakes
# pipeline and PR path. Listing that material here makes the lane policy ENFORCE
# that rule instead of offering a cheap route around it.

bin/fm-gate-refuse-lib.sh security-guards
bin/fm-arm-pretool-check.sh security-guards
bin/fm-arm-command-policy.mjs security-guards
bin/fm-cd-pretool-check.sh security-guards
bin/fm-cd-command-policy.mjs security-guards
bin/fm-subagent-pretool-check.sh security-guards
bin/fm-check-lib.sh security-guards
bin/fm-check-register.sh security-guards
bin/fm-lock.sh security-guards
bin/fm-lock-lib.sh security-guards
bin/fm-session-lock-lib.sh security-guards
bin/fm-afk-daemon-lock-lib.sh security-guards
bin/fm-lease.sh security-guards
bin/fm-lease-lib.sh security-guards

bin/fm-ff-lib.sh merge-routes
bin/fm-pr-check.sh merge-routes
bin/fm-pr-fitness-lib.sh merge-routes
bin/fm-pr-lib.sh merge-routes

bin/fm-landed-lib.sh teardown-and-discard
bin/fm-stash-guard.sh teardown-and-discard

# The policy's own enforcement points. The built-in catalog protects the files
# that DECLARE the routing policy; these are the two that RUN its dispatch gate,
# and a delivery that deleted the gate adds no lines, so every diff signal would
# read cheap and it would route itself past the gate it just removed.
#
# Do not delete these two as redundant with the bin/* entry below. That entry
# protects the gate only INCIDENTALLY, because this repository's shared tracked
# material happens to include bin/; a protection that stands because some list
# currently happens to include some directory is exactly the kind that
# disappears without anyone noticing. Narrowing this file is itself a critical
# surface, but that defends against DELIBERATE narrowing, not against narrowing
# that happens in passing inside a legitimate reorganisation.
# The surface NAME also carries real information. A row in the record saying
# routing-policy tells whoever reads it in six months that the delivery touched
# the policy's own enforcement point; shared-tracked-material does not, and the
# record is half the point of this policy.
bin/fm-spawn.sh routing-policy
bin/fm-promote.sh routing-policy

.github/workflows/* ci-configuration

# AGENTS.md section 1's shared tracked material, minus what a built-in surface
# already covers. bin/* makes every bin change here expensive, which is what
# AGENTS.md already requires today.
README.md shared-tracked-material
CONTRIBUTING.md shared-tracked-material
.tasks.toml shared-tracked-material
bin/* shared-tracked-material
.agents/skills/* shared-tracked-material
skills/* shared-tracked-material
